It matters only because the cause determines what can fix it.

If the satellites were overloaded by user count, the remedy would be more satellite capacity or fewer users. But at a crowded venue, the useful remedies are usually different:

- move where more sky is visible;
- wait for a fresh location fix;
- use a phone with newer multi-band/multi-constellation GNSS;
- avoid relying on congested cellular data for maps or assistance;
- download maps beforehand;
- recognize that indoor/stadium positioning may need Wi‑Fi, Bluetooth, or venue systems.

From a user’s perspective, “GPS is bad in crowds” is a perfectly reasonable shorthand. The satellite-versus-ecosystem distinction matters when explaining *why* it happens or deciding what to change.

No. They are not immune to physics, and “literally every human on Earth with zero consequence ever” would be an overstatement.

The key point is that the satellite already radiates a fixed signal into its coverage area. That signal has a fixed power, bandwidth, accuracy, footprint, and hardware cost. Those are real physical limits.

If another passive receiver listens, it does not make the satellite radiate more power. It only captures an extremely tiny portion of the radio energy that was already passing through its antenna area. The receiver doesn’t “drain” the signal in a meaningful way or send any request back.

So the real constraints are:

- finite satellite power and transmitter lifetime;
- weak GPS signals and limited coverage;
- limited navigation-message rate/content;
- signal blockage, reflections, and interference;
- the need for enough satellites in view for a position fix.

A useful analogy is sunlight: the Sun is not infinite or consequence-free, but one more person putting up a solar panel does not make the Sun work harder. Likewise, one more GPS receiver does not add meaningful load to a GPS satellite.

If every person used GPS at once, many phones and cellular/map services could struggle, and many receivers would have poor local reception. But the GPS satellites would still be broadcasting essentially the same signals at the same power as before.
